# Role-Based Experience Architecture (Mandatory)

NexClinic is **not** one dashboard with different permissions. It is specialized **workspaces** sharing one backend.

Each role must feel like a dedicated product: own homepage, dashboard, sidebar, widgets, KPIs, shortcuts, workflow, notifications, reports, and quick actions. Colors/terminology may differ when they reinforce purpose.

**Never** reuse the same dashboard across roles by hiding menu items. Shared PHP widgets/helpers are fine; shared role *experiences* are not.

## Workspaces (design independently)

| Workspace | Feels like | Purpose |
|-----------|------------|---------|
| Reception | Patient registration & queue system | Intake, appointments, queue, walk-in/emergency, fast search |
| Doctor | EMR | Consult, history, prescribe, lab orders/results, follow-ups, timeline |
| Nursing | Patient monitoring | Assigned patients, meds, vitals due, ward, transfers, checklists |
| Laboratory | Lab ops pipeline | Samples → processing → validation → release; TAT & critical results |
| Pharmacy | Pharmacy ERP | Dispense queue, inventory-first, low/expiring stock, suppliers |
| Accounting | Accounting software | Billing, payments, insurance, cash flow, invoices/receipts |
| Administration | Hospital ops center | Dept performance, beds, staff, operational KPIs, BI |
| Super Admin | SaaS platform console | Hospitals, users, licenses, health, audit, backups, config |

## UX principle

Reception must never feel like Doctor; Doctor never like Accountant; Pharmacist never like Administrator. Purpose must be obvious from layout, nav, language, widgets, and workflow on first paint.

## When building UI

- Start from the role's workspace homepage, not the classic shared admin shell
- Role-specific nav labels and primary actions (large/fast for reception; timeline-rich for clinical; inventory-first for pharmacy)
- If a feature spans roles, each role gets its own surface optimized for its job (e.g. doctor *issues* Rx; pharmacy *dispenses*)
